result.response:
The `null-guard` test now passes.

**Cause:** `displayName` in `packages/shared/src/format.js` did `return user.name.trim()` — it dereferenced `user` unconditionally, so `displayName(null)` threw `Cannot read properties of null (reading 'name')`, and `displayName({})` would have thrown on `undefined.trim()` next.

**Fix:** guard both the `user` object and its `name` field, falling back to `"anonymous"`:

```js
export function displayName(user) {
  const name = user?.name?.trim();
  return name ? name : "anonymous";
}
```

This also returns `"anonymous"` for a blank/whitespace-only name, which matches the test's intent (`{}` has no name → anonymous).

Note: the README states three tests fail intentionally — the other two (`cli-flag` in `packages/cli/src/flags.js`, and `timezone` in `packages/shared/src/dates.js`) are separate seeded failures, not side effects of this change. Say the word if you want those fixed too.

